PATCH 592: the oracle-replay instrument -- the engine's RNG, sim-step, render and mission-load seams
report to the shim, and the shim can drive them from the original's schedules.

board:0017.  Five hooks, each a one-line call into tools/native_main.c, inert unless an environment
variable names a file:

  0291  fist_rng_trace(result)   every LFSR draw (FIST_RNGTRACE: `rng <ax> t=<tick> from=<callers>`)
  c0e5  fist_sim_trace()         every sim step (`sim t=`), the per-step dumps (FIST_STEP0_DUMP /
                                 FIST_STEPN_DUMP / FIST_STEPEVERY_DUMP / FIST_STEP_DUMPLEN), the stop
                                 (FIST_STOP_AT_STEP) and the RNG schedule's polls (FIST_RNG_SCHEDULE:
                                 the oracle's poll count before each sim step, stepped here while the
                                 live poll 3920 -> [0x3a] = 0291 is muted in fist_icall_near)
  4754  fist_mission_trace()     the battle load = the sync point: FIST_RNG_SEED seeds the LFSR's
                                 pointer and four rolling words (DGROUP:0x1f82..0x1f8a) with the
                                 oracle's state there and loads the three schedules
  22dd  fist_render_trace()      every render (`render t=`); fist_phase_trace() after each 22f7
                                 dispatch (FIST_PHASE_SCHEDULE: the oracle's dispatches per render --
                                 the INT-8 budget word[0x450] is zeroed after that many, since the
                                 held INT-8s never expire it); fist_render_end_trace()
  459a  fist_frame_tick()        in place of the loop's cooperative pump (patch 295): with
                                 FIST_FRAME_SCHEDULE (the oracle's sim steps per render) the INT-8s
                                 are held (g_int8_replay, re_out/fist_vga.c) and fired here in the
                                 oracle's number; an explicit spin-wait pump (a fade) still gets its
                                 interrupts (g_int8_force)

The oracle's side is the patched DOSBox's FIST_REGTRACE at 0x11447/0x11454 (0291's returns, EAX =
the draw), 0x1d275 (c0e5), 0x24288 (the INT-8 ISR), 0x158e4 (4754), 0x1346d (22dd), 0x13487 (the
22f7 dispatch); tools/oracle/replay_sched.py turns its log into the seed and the three schedules,
tools/oracle/replay_cmp.py sets the two draw streams against each other per sim step.  With the
seed and the schedules the port draws the oracle's values as long as it draws the same number of
times: the first differing step names the diverging code.  Run on AZER1 (oracle run 13, 45 s, 2726
sim steps) it found the fourteen defects of patches 593-595, after which every draw and every
object-table byte of the 2726 steps match the oracle's.

